#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""rezztor-update: update a machine installed from the ISO from one signed file.

    rezztor-update                  the console: find a file, show it, install it, restart
    rezztor-update check FILE       say what a file is and whether it fits this machine
    rezztor-update install [FILE]   prepare the update beside the running version
    rezztor-update restart          switch to the prepared update; go back if it does not start
    rezztor-update discard          forget the file that was checked or prepared
    rezztor-update status           the state, as the Updates page shows it
    rezztor-update key              this machine's update key (Rezztor needs it once)
    rezztor-update serve            take the web application's request, if there is one (the unit runs this)
    rezztor-update bundle [usb]     make a support bundle for Rezztor; with usb, write it to a USB stick or disk
    rezztor-update reboot           restart the appliance (Debian updates of a package may need it)

A person supplies the file, or (spool actions refresh, download, update_all) the tool asks Rezztor's update
service for the next one; nothing else on this machine is fetched (ADR 0080 and the update-service ADR). A release
machine asks https://update.rezztor.com and trusts the system's CAs; only a UAT machine takes another address
and CA from the request. The file is a plain tar of three members, in this order:

    manifest.json   what it is, which versions it fits, what changes, restart or not
    manifest.sig    Ed25519 over manifest.json, base64, made with a release key
    payload.age     everything else, encrypted to this machine's own key; the manifest holds the
                    SHA-256 of what is inside

Trust is the public keys in /opt/rezztor/keys/*.pub and nothing else: no setting, no variable. This tool
checks the signature itself, with openssl, before it reads one field of the manifest, and it checks the
payload against the signed checksum before it unpacks anything. The web application can put a file and a
request into the inbox and read the state; it cannot make this tool install what Rezztor did not sign.

An update of the application's own files is not a new image in the file: it is the tree under /eagent,
and the new image is made here from the base release's image and that tree (`docker run`, `docker
commit`, no network). Every package is built on the base release, never on another package.

The switch is one line in the stack's .env, merged, never a new file. Before it the database is dumped;
after it the product must answer /api/health with the new version, or the old version comes back.

A package of format 4 (ADR 0092) may also carry the network guard, systemd units from a fixed list, and Debian's
updates since its core: .debs and their index under os/, covered by the signed checksum like the rest. Those are
installed only after the new version answered, with apt reading that folder and no other source, so going back
to the old version never has an operating system to take back. A failure there leaves the new version running and
is said; the next package carries the same updates again. Whether the appliance then needs a restart is decided
here, from what changed (REBOOT) and from /run/reboot-required; the restart itself waits for an administrator.

Everything that touches the machine is in the class Host. Tests replace it.
"""

